Box 2.2. National FLW strategies and the FAO Food Waste Hierarchy
The Voluntary Code of Conduct for Food Loss and Waste Reduction (CoC FLW), a global non-legally binding instrument developed by the FAO, was endorsed in June 2021. The CoC FLW presents internationally and regionally recognised, locally and nationally adaptable guiding principles and standards for responsible practices that governments and other stakeholders can voluntarily apply to reduce FLW while promoting sustainable and inclusive agricultural and agrifood systems.
The CoC FLW proposes a Food Waste Hierarchy to provide guidance on how different material streams can be handled to address FLW (Figure 2.8). Such a hierarchy can be used as part of national FLW strategies and legislations to organise activities in reducing and managing FLW and to mobilise all food supply chain stakeholders. For instance, Colombian Law No. 1.990/2019 creates the environment to prevent food loss and waste; among its provisions, it prioritises actions that divert food otherwise lost or wasted to human consumption and, secondarily, to animal feed.
Figure 2.8. Alignment of FLW national strategies with the Food Waste Hierarchy
